            - Edit: replace the three sentences in Loop D / Observe with the saved-grid values, rounded to 1 decimal place. Suggested wording: "Under p=0.50 we reject 3-5% of the time (the discrete two-sided exact test is slightly conservative). At p=0.52: 4%, 7%, 23%. At p=0.55: 8%, 26%, 88%. At p=0.60: 23%, 79%, ~100%."
        - HIGH (applied): "When the truth is exactly fair (p = 0.50), we reject in roughly 5% of experiments at every N. That is the type I error rate, and it equals alpha." The exact two-sided binomial test is **conservative** because the support is discrete; the actual rate at alpha=0.05 is 3-5% depending on N (3.3% at N=50, 4.0% at N=200, 4.6% at N=1000). Saying "equals alpha" is a pedagogical trap.
            - Edit: change to "the type I error rate **is at most alpha**. For a discrete test like the binomial, it can be strictly less than alpha; here we see roughly 3-5% rather than exactly 5%, because no integer cutoff hits 5% exactly."
        - HIGH (applied): Loop A says "Counts below 40 or above 60 happen, but only about 5% of the time combined." The combined tail probability P(X<40 or X>60 | p=0.5, N=100) is 3.52%, not 5%. The 5% is the alpha threshold, not the realized tail mass.
            - Edit: "Counts at or below 39 or at or above 61 happen only about 3.5% of the time combined. That is the tail mass we will call 'rejection region' once we set alpha = 0.05; it is below the 5% budget because no integer cutoff hits it exactly."
        - HIGH (applied): Loop B Observe: "At N = 100, the curves track each other almost perfectly. Both flag 'reject' at roughly the same observed counts." This conflates two-sided p-value with one-sided posterior P(p>0.5). The reason the boundaries line up at k=60/61 is a coincidence of (two-sided alpha = 0.05) versus (one-sided posterior > 0.95) under a symmetric prior, not a deep convergence. At k=60 the two-sided p-value is 0.057 (do NOT reject) while P(p>0.5)=0.977 (Bayesian rejects).
            - Edit: rephrase to flag the apples-to-oranges comparison explicitly: "At N=100 the boundaries roughly coincide, but only because we paired a *two-sided* frequentist test (which spends alpha equally on both tails) with a *one-sided* posterior probability (P(p>0.5)). They are answering different questions; that they cross their respective thresholds at nearby observations is a feature of the symmetric prior, not a general law. The right Bayesian counterpart to a two-sided test is something like 2*min(P(p<0.5), P(p>0.5)) or comparing the credible interval to {0.5}."
        - HIGH (applied): Loop B Probe: "Beta(2, 8) prior. With 6/10 we end up even more confident in bias." Beta(2,8) has mean 0.2; with 6 heads / 10 tosses the posterior is Beta(8,12), mean 0.4, P(p>0.5)=0.18. We are LESS confident the coin is biased high; we end up *split* about which direction. The claim is wrong as written.
            - Edit: replace with: "Beta(2, 8) is a strong prior that says 'I expect heads-rate around 0.2'. With 6/10 the posterior is Beta(8,12), mean 0.4 and P(p>0.5) ~ 0.18: data drags belief up but the prior anchors it below 0.5. With enough N the data dominates and we agree with the flat-prior result."
        - MEDIUM (applied): Loop A Bayesian: "the Bayesian is sure that p sits inside a particular interval". A 95% credible interval is a probability statement about p **conditional on the prior and model**, not a claim of certainty. The contrast with the frequentist sentence is fine but the word "sure" reads as overclaim.
            - Edit: change "the Bayesian is sure that p sits inside a particular interval" to "the Bayesian assigns 95% posterior probability that p sits inside a particular interval (given the prior and model)".
        - MEDIUM (applied): Loop A: "alpha is the rate of *false positives*. If the coin really is fair, alpha is the fraction of experiments that will mislead us into saying 'rigged'." For a continuous test this is true; for the discrete binomial it is an **upper bound** ("size <= alpha"). Tighten to match the HIGH item above.
            - Edit: "alpha is an *upper bound* on the false-positive rate. If the coin is fair, at most a fraction alpha of experiments will mislead us; for a discrete test it is often strictly less."
